#File indexing system
    To solve this assignment I needed several data structures:
1. A doubly linked list for managing files. For each file, I store its id, its relevance score, a singly linked list
with its associated keywords, and pointers to the previous and next file.
2. A singly linked list holding keywords (the ones associated with a file)
3. A multiway retrieval tree, where the left pointer links to the first child and the right pointer to the next sibling. Then, for each node
I have in "c" the character it holds, a variable "t" marking whether the node is terminal or not, and for terminal nodes, a singly
linked list with the files that have that word in their word list
4. A singly linked reference list, in which, as mentioned above, I keep for each word the files that contain it
5. A max-heap in which I placed the references obtained from the tree, with priority based on the file's score and, in case of a tie, the file
with the lexicographically smaller identifier
    I also needed several functions to solve all the requirements, including:
- Some for allocating/initializing the structures: Alocarefisier, InitArb, Nod, Alocareheap, and for freeing memory:
DistrLcuv, DistrArb, DistrLfis and Freemem
- Others for actually fulfilling the requirements:
1. Addchild:
    This function adds a child node to a parent node (if it's the first child we put it at left, otherwise we go through the children so the
siblings stay in lexicographic order)
2. Insarb:
    The function walks the multiway tree and if it doesn't find the character it creates a node for it and adds it, and if it happens to be a 
terminal node, it adds a reference to the corresponding file
3. ADD
    The function adds a new file to the system. First I check whether the file already exists, and if so, I print the error message and stop,
and if not, I put it at the end of the file list, and for each word I check whether it's already in the file's word list, and if not I add it
both to the tree (calling the previous function) and to the word list, and at the end I print ok, i.e. the message reflecting that the
operation succeeded
4. Delcuv
    The function deletes an entire word from the tree. It recursively searches for the terminal character and if it has no reference to a file,
it means we will delete it, and in practice we always delete the last character of the word, and we do this until the whole word is deleted
5. DEL
    The function deletes a file from the system. It looks it up by id, then removes its reference from the tree. For each keyword, I walk the
tree and remove the reference to the file. Then I check whether that node happens to be left with no reference to a file, and if so, I call 
the previous function to remove the whole word. After that, I actually remove the file from the file list, and if the file wasn't found, I 
print this.
6. ADDKW
    The function adds a keyword to a file. I check whether the file exists and then whether it already has that word, and if so I print
ok, otherwise I add the word to the word list, call the Insarb function to add it to the tree as well, and print Ok. If I don't find the file,
I print this.
7. DELKW
    The function removes a keyword from a file. I look up the file by id, then the word. I walk the tree until I reach the terminal node of
the word, remove the reference to the file; if it's left with no reference at all, I call Delcuv to delete the whole word, then remove the word
from the file's word list and print OK. If I have the file but not the word, I again print ok, and if I don't find the file, I print this.
8. FIND
    The function prints all files that contain a given keyword. I walk the multiway tree until I find the terminal node of that word.
Then I count the number of files I have references to, and afterward I put the ids of those files into a vector. Using the qsort function, 
which calls a function that sorts characters lexicographically, I sort the vector of file ids lexicographically, so that afterward I can print 
them. If I have files that don't contain any keywords, I remove them from the file list.
9. RelMaxHeap
    The function is used to compare heap elements, based on score, or, in case of a tie, by the lexicographically
smaller identifier
10. Push up/Push down/Inserare/Extragere
    These are functions that move an element up/down and insert/extract an element from a heap
11. TOPK
    The function finds the k most relevant files associated with a keyword. I walk the tree to the desired terminal node, count its
reference files, allocate the heap and put the associated files into it, then print only the first k files from the heap, or all of them if 
their number is less than k.
12. Parcurgere
    The function walks the multiway tree and builds, each time, the word along the "branch" we're on. If it happens to be a terminal node it 
means the word is complete and we save it. Then it continues the same process on the node's children/sibling.
13. PRINT
    The function prints all the keywords in the multiway tree. First, by calling the previous function, I put all the words from the tree into 
a vector. Then, since the words in the vector are already guaranteed to be in lexicographic order, I do the printing. I go through the tree again and,
once I reach a terminal node, I count its associated files, and take their ids and put them into a vector. That vector I sort lexicographically
as well, then I print the word, the number of files associated with it, and the ids from the vector, in lexicographic order.
14. Parc
    The function recursively walks (through children and then through the sibling) the subtree of a node, and whenever it finds a terminal node, 
it walks its list of reference files. Their ids I put into a vector, but I always make sure they haven't already been added.
15. PREFIX
    The function determines all files that contain at least one keyword starting with a given prefix. I walk the tree until I reach the node of
the last character of the prefix, and if it happens to be terminal, I keep its files, and I call the previous function to walk its whole subtree, 
if it has children. Then, the vector containing the file ids is sorted lexicographically with qsort and the comparison function, and I print 
the number of files and then their ids. If I happen not to find the character while searching, I print empty.
    In main I initialize the file list and the multiway tree, then read the number of operations and call the Resolve_task function. This function
reads the command from input and, depending on it, calls one of the functions ADD (for this I build a vector of words containing all the words
associated with a file), DEL, ADDKW, DELKW, FIND, TOPK, PRINT, PREFIX. Finally, I free all the memory by calling the Freemem function.